Hmm — but with many objects the window widens: List returns N entries, then verifyObject reads them one by one unlocked. If I create, say, 50 objects of a few MB each, verify takes a while reading them all unlocked; meanwhile another goroutine puts new versions of all 50 names and gcs — deleting all 50 old objects. Then verify hits missing objects. That's very likely to fail with old code and pass with new.

